{
  "strategy": "Ground the final static diagram frame that contains the last shape, then use structured visual analysis plus OCR to identify the shape and the on-screen prompt. The refiner should determine traceability by counting odd-degree vertices from the grounded diagram rather than guessing from the options.",
  "tool_calls": [
    {
      "step": 1,
      "tool": "frame_retriever",
      "arguments": {
        "video_path": "VIDEO",
        "query": "static black-and-white line drawing showing the last shape, with a circle connected to a diamond and a horizontal line across it, alongside the prompt asking if it is possible to trace the shape",
        "timestamps": null,
        "num_frames": 3
      },
      "purpose": "Retrieve candidate frames for the final question-bearing diagram state, since the answer depends on the last shape rather than the earlier gold tracing example.",
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "step": 2,
      "tool": "chart_analyzer",
      "arguments": {
        "frame_path": "<STEP_1:frames>",
        "timestamp": null,
        "query": "describe the full structure of the black-and-white line diagram, including all visible vertices, closed shapes, intersections, and which line segments connect at each point"
      },
      "purpose": "Obtain a structured reading of the last shape so the refiner can identify the graph topology and count vertex degrees from the actual diagram.",
      "depends_on": [
        1
      ]
    },
    {
      "step": 3,
      "tool": "ocr",
      "arguments": {
        "frame_path": "<STEP_1:frames>",
        "timestamp": null
      },
      "purpose": "Verify the on-screen prompt and confirm that the retrieved frames correspond to the final asked shape, not an explanatory or earlier tracing frame.",
      "depends_on": [
        1
      ]
    }
  ],
  "refinement_instructions": "Write the trace from scratch. Use step 3 only to anchor that the retrieved frame is the final question screen about whether the shape can be traced. Use the frame-level results from step 2, not just any top-level summary, to describe the actual line structure of the last diagram. In the rewritten trace, explicitly identify the vertices where segments meet, count how many connected lines meet at each relevant point, and determine how many vertices have odd degree. Then apply the Euler-path rule: a figure can be traced in one stroke without retracing iff it has exactly 0 or 2 odd-degree vertices; 0 odd vertices means a closed tracing, 2 odd vertices means an open tracing. Choose the option whose explanation matches the grounded odd-vertex count. Do not infer the answer from option wording alone; derive it from the grounded diagram structure. If the chart_analyzer output is partially ambiguous about one attachment or intersection, state that ambiguity explicitly and avoid overclaiming beyond what the grounded structure supports."
}
